⚙️ Working Principle

The system operates according to the following sequence:

1. The PIR sensor monitors its detection area.
2. When movement is detected, the sensor sends a HIGH signal to Arduino digital pin 2.
3. The Arduino reads the sensor output.
4. The Arduino activates the relay through digital pin 8.
5. The relay switches ON the connected light.
6. The Arduino records the most recent motion signal.
7. When the PIR output remains LOW for the programmed interval, the Arduino deactivates the relay.
8. The light switches OFF automatically.

The program uses a 10-second timer, which can be adjusted according to project requirements.

🧠 Arduino Program

// PIR Motion Sensor Based Automatic Light Control
// Arduino UNO + PIR Sensor + Relay Module

const int PIR_PIN = 2;
const int RELAY_PIN = 8;

// Assumes an active-LOW relay module
const int RELAY_ON = LOW;
const int RELAY_OFF = HIGH;

unsigned long lastMotionTime = 0;
const unsigned long lightDelay = 10000;

bool lightIsOn = false;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);

  pinMode(PIR_PIN, INPUT);
  pinMode(RELAY_PIN, OUTPUT);

  digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, RELAY_OFF);

  Serial.println("PIR Automatic Light System Started");
}

void loop() {
  int pirState = digitalRead(PIR_PIN);

  if (pirState == HIGH) {
    lastMotionTime = millis();

    if (!lightIsOn) {
      Serial.println("Motion detected: Light ON");
      lightIsOn = true;
    }

    digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, RELAY_ON);
  }

  if (lightIsOn &&
      millis() - lastMotionTime >= lightDelay) {

    digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, RELAY_OFF);
    lightIsOn = false;

    Serial.println("No motion: Light OFF");
  }

  delay(100);
}

Important: This code assumes the relay module is active-LOW. For an active-HIGH relay, change the relay settings to:

const int RELAY_ON = HIGH;
const int RELAY_OFF = LOW;

The PIR sensor's output timing and retrigger settings can affect the actual light-off time.

🚀 How to Run the Project

1. Install Arduino IDE on your computer.
2. Connect the Arduino UNO using a USB cable.
3. Open Arduino IDE.
4. Select Tools → Board → Arduino AVR Boards → Arduino UNO.
5. Select the correct port under Tools → Port.
6. Create a new sketch.
7. Copy the Arduino program into the sketch.
8. Click Verify to compile the program.
9. Click Upload to upload the program to the Arduino.
10. Connect the PIR sensor and relay module according to the connection tables.
11. Power the system and allow the PIR sensor to stabilize.
12. Move in front of the sensor to test the automatic light control.
13. Open Tools → Serial Monitor and set the baud rate to 9600 to observe the system messages.

